1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:21,787
By noon the opera house belonged to Shawna, contained forty-seven
people Vesper wanted alive, and had sold out a concert my wife hadn't
agreed to give yet. "Nigga, how you sell tickets before the singer
says yes?" I asked the manager. The manager, a round man named Paolo,
held his hat against his chest.

2
00:00:22,037 --> 00:00:44,895
"We heard she was coming." "Nigga, she came to look at curtains." "The
city would pay to see that also." I looked at Shawna, then back at
him. "This nigga got taste and no manners. Baby, come decide what part
we keeping." Paolo nodded so eagerly I almost liked him before my wife
had approved the performance. Shawna looked up from the stage.

3
00:00:45,145 --> 00:01:07,966
"What time?" "Eight." "Get me a piano." Nigga, that was the entire
negotiation. Rosa stood beside her in rehearsal clothes, looking out
over the velvet seats. "I want the second song." "You can have three."
"I knew this marriage would eventually benefit me." Tank sat in the
front row with his feet spread so far nobody else could use it.

4
00:01:08,217 --> 00:01:29,479
He watched Rosa cross the stage like the theater had been built to
solve his particular eyesight. "That nigga in love," I told Shawna.
"That nigga has been in love. He just finally stopped looking
surprised." Vesper's people occupied the dressing rooms upstairs.
Assistants. Drivers. Two singers.

5
00:01:29,729 --> 00:01:54,433
An old cook who had brought her own knives because she didn't trust
palace kitchens she couldn't insult personally. No prisoners in
chains. People who had served one house so long that leaving it felt
like stepping out of their own names. Vesper moved among them,
checking rooms, carrying bags, doing little practical things with a
seriousness nobody had ever applauded her for.

6
00:01:54,683 --> 00:02:17,728
I let her work. Paolo caught me before I reached the door. Captain.
One small difficulty. Nigga, don't introduce a large problem wearing a
little hat. What is it? He pointed up. The stage curtain had caught
halfway open. A man on the high walkway was pulling at a rope with
both hands while another shouted that he was making it worse.

7
00:02:17,978 --> 00:02:42,420
Below them, Shawna stood with her arms folded, waiting for the theater
to stop taking rehearsal personally. Has it always done that? I asked.
Only for important performances. Nigga, it better retire that habit
today. I went up the side stairs. At the top, the two stagehands
stared at my badge, then at my face. Captain, you don't have to— One
leaned toward the other.

8
00:02:42,670 --> 00:03:06,164
That nigga fixes curtains too? Nigga, that man's wife downstairs. This
nigga can hear you, I told them. And that part about my wife is why
the curtain losing today. Show me what's caught. The first stagehand
pointed with both hands. The other took the loose rope and got ready
to help instead of spending the whole rehearsal admiring the
volunteer.

9
00:03:06,414 --> 00:03:28,642
The curtain's heavy edge had twisted around its guide. I got both
hands on it, felt the stubborn weight, and pulled until the trapped
fabric came loose with a long velvet sigh. The curtain opened. Below
us, the whole stage filled with light. Shawna looked up at me from the
piano. You showing off up there? Nigga fixing your sky.

10
00:03:28,892 --> 00:03:51,649
She laughed and played the first chord. Her voice came up through the
theater, clear and warm, and the two stagehands forgot to look
impressed with me because my wife had begun doing her part. I let
them. When I came downstairs, Paolo shook my hand as if we'd concluded
a matter of enormous artistic importance. Perfect, he said.

11
00:03:51,898 --> 00:04:14,388
I looked at Shawna singing beneath the open curtain. Nigga, I know.
Bastien met me at the stage door with Marcellus. Both wore dark suits.
Both looked like they had decided to attend a funeral and were willing
to arrange one if necessary. "Nigga, y'all dress like the portrait
owes you money." "In a sense," Marcellus said.

12
00:04:14,638 --> 00:04:38,377
Shawna came down the steps and straightened my collar. "Bring back the
book." "That's all?" "And yourself. The book can't come to my show
alone." I kissed her. "Front row." "Better be." The House of Nine
Doors looked worse in daylight. Nigga, I had done quality work. One
balcony still had tire marks across the marble. The gallery doors were
boarded.

13
00:04:38,627 --> 00:05:02,793
Somebody had placed flowers near the broken orchestra platform, and I
couldn't tell whether they were mourning the musicians' equipment or
the baron's dignity. Police guarded the front entrance. Black Mantle
soldiers guarded the back. Zero's men. Different uniforms, same rich-
man posture: shoulders wide because somebody else had promised to pay
for the consequences.

14
00:05:03,043 --> 00:05:26,700
Ardent stood between both groups looking like his afternoon had become
legally confusing. He brightened when he saw me. "Captain." "Nigga,
who invited the funeral jackets?" "The emperor claims an interest in
the property." "I claimed it first. With evidence. And a Ferrari." A
Black Mantle officer approached. Tall. Blond. A little scar beside his
mouth.

15
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:50,251
He introduced himself as Colonel Severin Drake, forty-six, commander
of Zero's household forces, like any of that was supposed to make me
stop standing where I was. "The house is closed." I showed him my
badge. "Open." "Your foreign rank has no authority here." Ardent
straightened. "The captain acts with my full cooperation." Drake
looked at him.

16
00:05:50,501 --> 00:06:15,218
"You should be more careful with that." "Nigga, you should be more
careful with your tone," I said. "This man been nothing but handsome
and helpful." Ardent's younger officers tried not to smile. Drake
stepped closer. "You enjoy making people love you." "Nigga, you make
that sound difficult. Look at this man." I nodded toward Ardent.
"Useful. Respectful. Helped my wife get home.

17
00:06:15,468 --> 00:06:39,921
Nigga, I liked him in twelve seconds. You been here longer and somehow
getting worse." One of Ardent's officers coughed into his fist. Drake
looked at him. "That nigga ain't your problem," I said. "You
threatening the wrong part of the conversation." Bastien walked past
us toward the front door. Drake moved to stop him. I caught Drake's
wrist. Not hard enough to injure him.

18
00:06:40,171 --> 00:06:57,126
Hard enough for his face to understand the rest of the sentence. "Let
the old nigga walk." For a moment all the guns around us seemed to
remember what they were for. Then Drake stepped back. "You have
fifteen minutes." "Nigga, I was gonna take twenty.

19
00:06:57,376 --> 00:07:20,472
You just made it thirty." Inside, the house smelled of smoke, polish,
and rich people pretending damage wasn't permanent if they could
afford repairs. Bastien led us through a long corridor into a portrait
room I hadn't reached during the car chase. King Adrien Valcour hung
above a fireplace in a gold frame. He wore the Crown of Midnight.

20
00:07:20,722 --> 00:07:43,922
For the first time, I got a good look at the thing we had crossed a
sea to take from Zero. Black diamonds. White gold. Seven rising
points. A blue stone at the front dark enough to look like midnight
had learned how to shine. "That's a crown," I said. Marcellus smiled.
"Yes." "Nigga, I was expecting a crown, but that's a crown.

21
00:07:44,172 --> 00:08:08,822
Why nobody tell me the nigga had that much hat?" "We've discussed it
several times." "Words, my nigga. You used words. Should've held your
hands this far apart and looked worried about my neck." Bastien stood
beneath the portrait. "His nose was wrong." Eliane's letter came back
to me. The old royal face. The face whose nose was wrong. Marcellus
studied the frame.

22
00:08:09,072 --> 00:08:32,514
Bastien looked at the fireplace. I looked at both men looking until I
decided we were giving the wall too much time to feel important. "Can
I take it down?" "Carefully," Marcellus said. Nigga, I took it down
carefully enough that only one corner of the wall objected. Behind the
portrait was a shallow bronze door. No glitter. No dramatic ticking.

23
00:08:32,764 --> 00:08:53,452
Just a family crest worn smooth where somebody's hand had once touched
it often. Bastien took a key from inside his coat. I looked at him.
"You had that the whole time?" "I knew where it belonged. I was afraid
to leave her alone with what opening it would bring." "Nigga, she got
company now." The key turned.

24
00:08:53,702 --> 00:09:18,006
Inside sat a small black book, a red ribbon, and a child's drawing old
enough that the paper had browned. A little boy had drawn a ship and a
man with a crown too large for his head. Nico's name was written at
the bottom. Bastien touched the edge of the paper with one finger.
Marcellus lifted the book. Names. Payments. Promises. Not magic.

25
00:09:18,256 --> 00:09:39,227
Worse, in a way: men writing down what they would do to other men if
enough money reached the right table. Eliane's stitched marks matched
the page corners. Marcellus found Rafael Sorel's name three times
before I heard footsteps in the corridor. "Time," I said. Drake stood
in the doorway with six men.

26
00:09:39,477 --> 00:10:03,878
"You have recovered imperial property." "Nigga, you timed that
beautifully." "Give it to me." Bastien closed the bronze door.
Marcellus slipped the book inside his jacket. I picked up the
portrait. "You want your old king back?" Drake raised his gun. I threw
King Adrien Valcour at him. Nigga, the king finally did something
useful after sixteen years on the wall.

27
00:10:04,128 --> 00:10:25,689
The frame struck Drake across the shoulder. His shot went into the
ceiling. I drove forward before his men could get a clear line, hit
the nearest one hard enough to put him into a cabinet, and heard
Bastien's pistol answer from beside the fireplace. Marcellus ducked
behind a table. Ardent's officers shouted from the front hall.

28
00:10:25,939 --> 00:10:50,356
The house filled with the same noise it had made two nights earlier,
and I started to understand why rich people kept extra mansions. Drake
pulled free of the broken frame and came at me with a knife. He was
good. Not Gault strong. Not red-mask fast. Precise. Every movement
meant something ugly and arrived without introduction. He cut my
sleeve. I hit his jaw.

29
00:10:50,606 --> 00:11:11,156
He drove me against the fireplace. I caught his wrist before the knife
found anything I couldn't replace with a tailor. "You think they love
you?" he hissed. "Nigga, you asking while we wrestle? Pick a mood." I
slammed him into the mantel. The knife fell. Bastien fired over my
shoulder at a man rushing the doorway.

30
00:11:11,406 --> 00:11:33,970
I shoved Drake into the broken portrait and went after Marcellus. The
front hall was blocked. The gallery was burning again. Nigga, that
house had an unfortunate relationship with my visits. "Back way,"
Bastien said. "That's where his men are." "Not all the way back. The
ninth door." I stared at him. "The wall door?" He nodded.

31
00:11:34,220 --> 00:11:57,387
Tank's earlier threat about taking the whole wall came to mind, and I
decided my brother deserved a chance to be right at a distance. We ran
upstairs. The little blue door opened onto nothing. Nothing, in this
case, meant twenty feet of air above the harbor, a narrow stone ledge,
and a maintenance boat tied below. Marcellus looked down.

32
00:11:57,637 --> 00:12:21,561
"I am sixty-two." "Nigga, you wearing it beautifully. Come here." I
helped him through onto the ledge. Bastien followed. Gunfire struck
the door behind us. I kicked it shut, looked at the boat, and called
down to its startled driver. "Move left!" "What?" "Nigga, your other
left!" He moved. We jumped. The boat rocked so hard the harbor tried
to get in with us.

33
00:12:21,811 --> 00:12:44,846
Marcellus landed against a coil of rope. Bastien hit the bench and
rolled. I came down last, caught the boat's canopy, and tore half of
it loose before my feet found the deck. The driver stared. "Captain
Aiden?" "Drive, my nigga. Autographs when the roof stops shooting."
The boat shot away from the house as bullets struck the water behind
us.

34
00:12:45,096 --> 00:13:09,792
Ardent's patrol launch cut across the harbor to meet us. His officers
opened the way. Above, on the balcony I'd driven through before, Drake
stood with blood at his mouth and the king's torn portrait tangled
around one boot. I waved. Nigga, manners matter when you leave
somebody's house. We reached the opera house at seven forty-three.
Shawna was furious. Not because I'd been late.

35
00:13:10,042 --> 00:13:32,250
Because I'd been early enough for her to see the sleeve. "You
bleeding?" "Mostly architecture." "Aiden." "Nigga fought a portrait.
Portrait lost. Little cut. Nothing interesting." "Then let your wife
be interested before you start explaining the nigga in the frame." I
held out my arm. Marcellus quietly held up the book behind me.

36
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:43,486
"At least one of you came back with all his corners," she said.
"Nigga, I came back with the useful ones." She checked it herself,
cleaned it, and put a fresh shirt in my hands.

37
00:13:43,736 --> 00:14:06,271
"You have twelve minutes." "To explain?" "To sit down and look like my
husband." "Nigga, that's my strongest subject." "Then get an A and
keep your feet out of the aisle." I did it in nine. Nigga, greatness
respects showtime. The opera house filled until people stood along the
walls. Vesper's household sat together in one balcony.

38
00:14:06,521 --> 00:14:30,002
Ardent and his officers occupied another, their uniforms bright
beneath the chandeliers. Gault arrived in a dark suit with no chain.
When he saw me, he raised a hand and the whole row around him stood to
make room. Tank sat beside me with Rosa's engagement ring box in his
pocket, though she'd already said yes on the island. He liked carrying
it.

39
00:14:30,252 --> 00:14:52,559
Said it reminded him he had managed one proposal without anybody
shooting. The lights went down. Shawna walked onto the stage. No
crown. No gun. No car. Just my wife, a piano, and a room that had no
idea how quiet she could make it. She sang the first song without
looking for me. That was what I loved about it.

40
00:14:52,809 --> 00:15:12,747
She wasn't borrowing courage from my face. She had brought her own. By
the second verse, a woman behind me was crying. By the final note,
even the waiters had stopped moving. The applause came a second late
because everybody needed a moment to remember they had hands. Nigga, I
stood first.

41
00:15:12,997 --> 00:15:36,543
I stood so fast Tank stood with me on instinct, and the rest of the
room followed like my wife's voice had pulled the city onto its feet.
She found me then. Smiled. Everything I'd stolen, won, survived, or
driven through felt small enough to put in a pocket beside that look.
Rosa took the stage for the second song. Tank forgot to sit down.

42
00:15:36,793 --> 00:15:58,968
She pointed at him once during the chorus, and the nigga actually
touched his own chest like he needed confirmation she meant him.
Mercedes leaned over from the next row. "That's your woman, nigga.
Stop looking adopted by surprise." He laughed, embarrassed and proud,
and I put an arm around his shoulders while the whole theater cheered
her name.

43
00:15:59,218 --> 00:16:20,918
"That's your nigga singing," I told him. "I know." "Then let the woman
see you know." Tank raised both hands over his head and shouted her
name so loudly Rosa almost lost the next line laughing. "There that
nigga go," Mercedes said. "Whole damn balcony heard his relationship,"
Porsche answered. Nigga, so did Rosa.

44
00:16:21,168 --> 00:16:46,549
She found her note again and sent it straight back to him. Upstairs
afterward, Marcellus laid the recovered ledger beside Eliane's coat.
Vesper read in silence. Payments to the harbor commander. Orders
transferring the old king's properties before the fire. Names of men
who had sworn loyalty at a funeral for people they'd helped kill. Then
she reached the last page and stopped.

45
00:16:46,799 --> 00:17:10,873
"What?" I asked. She turned the book toward me. A newer sheet had been
tucked inside the old binding. Bell must have added it after acquiring
the house. A record of the coming tournament's final wager. The Crown
was to be awarded publicly. Then the winner would be invited aboard
Zero's armored yacht for a private coronation. No winner was meant to
return.

46
00:17:11,123 --> 00:17:29,271
Tank stared at the page. "Nigga, they got a whole funeral scheduled
after your trophy." I looked at Shawna standing in the doorway, still
wearing the dress the city had just applauded. Then I looked at
Vesper. "Aight," I said. "We gonna need a bigger after-party."
